{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01AHnAs9PyLPr6YKkuvJiuV5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/**\n2\t * Utilities for variable-length lists, so we don't always have to use fixed-\n3\t * length arrays.\n4\t * The two extreme approaches are linked lists, which are fast to resize but\n5\t * slow to access (and for that matter, bad for memory management), and fixed-\n6\t * size arrays, which are fast to access but slow to resize. We can go for an\n7\t * intermediate approach with a linked list *of* fixed-size arrays to compromise\n8\t * and get the middlest of both worlds.\n9\t */\n10\t\n11\t#pragma once\n12\t\n13\tstruct List;\n14\ttypedef\n15\t    struct List\n16\t    List;\n17\t\n18\t// Make and destroy lists.\n19\tstruct List* create_list(int blocksize);\n20\tint destroy_list(List* l);\n21\t\n22\t// Access the n-th element of a list.\n23\tvoid* lget_element(List* l, int index);\n24\t// Add new element. Returns success (0) or failure (-1).\n25\tint ladd_element(List* l, void* element);\n26\t// Set n-th element.\n27\tint lset_element(List* l, int index, void* value);\n28\t// Iterate over elements. This takes a highly customizable approach. We pass in\n29\t// a function which takes a void* pointer and returns an int, and we pass this\n30\t// function over every void* element in the list. We return the sum of all\n31\t// return values thus accumulated.\n32\tint literate(List* l, int (*fn)(void*));\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f945789d-e75c-4c84-b149-bd04f12e2f57","uuid":"7d041a57-6a1f-4b3a-ae0a-45a2cd7aab3c","timestamp":"2026-05-28T19:48:29.427Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/harvest-crust-a8Kr5m/project/c_src/src/util/list.h","content":"/**\n * Utilities for variable-length lists, so we don't always have to use fixed-\n * length arrays.\n * The two extreme approaches are linked lists, which are fast to resize but\n * slow to access (and for that matter, bad for memory management), and fixed-\n * size arrays, which are fast to access but slow to resize. We can go for an\n * intermediate approach with a linked list *of* fixed-size arrays to compromise\n * and get the middlest of both worlds.\n */\n\n#pragma once\n\nstruct List;\ntypedef\n    struct List\n    List;\n\n// Make and destroy lists.\nstruct List* create_list(int blocksize);\nint destroy_list(List* l);\n\n// Access the n-th element of a list.\nvoid* lget_element(List* l, int index);\n// Add new element. Returns success (0) or failure (-1).\nint ladd_element(List* l, void* element);\n// Set n-th element.\nint lset_element(List* l, int index, void* value);\n// Iterate over elements. This takes a highly customizable approach. We pass in\n// a function which takes a void* pointer and returns an int, and we pass this\n// function over every void* element in the list. We return the sum of all\n// return values thus accumulated.\nint literate(List* l, int (*fn)(void*));\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}}}
